+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Strategy
+
+## Context
+
+In offline field work, a single visit (plot observation) can be edited independently by multiple workers after they compare field notes or correct errors. Tablets may be disconnected for 48 hours, so edits can arrive days after their starting state was fetched. The server must synthesize these independent submissions into a current record without discarding any original submission — a standing tenet of the HLD.
+
+The concurrency strategy determines what a "current record" means when competing edits exist: Do we preserve each submission as a complete scientific unit and require manual review of conflicts, or do we infer which field edits are unrelated, combine them automatically, and review only overlaps? This choice shapes the submission envelope (what clients send), the review queue (what coordinators see), and the export eligibility rules (when a record is ready for the partner CSV).
+
+Tablet-sync and review-export depend entirely on this decision. Neither can proceed with their own design until this choice is made and its contract is stable.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Inherited gate**: All submissions must be retained permanently. Silent discards are prohibited (HLD tenet: *do not silently discard a submitted edit*). Neither option is eliminated by this gate, but both must preserve originals.
+
+**Major criterion — Review workload**: How many submitted edits surface as review work that coordinators must see and resolve? Review work is necessary (no option eliminates it), but review volume directly affects coordinator capacity. The team has two coordinators; a Friday export deadline creates a hard bound on how much can be reviewed. Designs that surface more edits as review work require either more staffing, faster resolution workflows, or accepting that some edits won't make the Friday export.
+
+**Major criterion — Implementation and maintenance burden**: How complex is the logic that determines what gets merged and what requires review? This includes the merge rules themselves (which field combinations make sense), the error handling when rules don't cover a case, and the long-term cost of maintaining merge correctness as the data model evolves. Rules that work for scalar fields may fail for observer lists or species entries. New fields may be added; existing categories may change.
+
+**Major criterion — Preservation of scientific intent**: Can the system distinguish between unrelated field edits (correcting an observer name and adding a missing species) and related edits (revising a species ID and its cover estimate)? Field names alone don't encode relationships. The system cannot infer all such relationships without domain knowledge or explicit metadata. Automatically merging across ununderstood relationships risks combining observations that were scientifically separate.
+
+**Moderate criterion — Downstream contract clarity**: How much machinery do tablet-sync and review-export need to implement? Simpler contracts are easier to specify, test, and eventually hand off to maintainers. Complex contracts with many state transitions increase the surface area for bugs.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each submission carries a base-revision ID (the revision the worker started from) and a complete new observation state. The server compares the base-revision ID to the current record's revision ID. If they match, the submission advances the current revision. If they don't match (the worker started from stale state), the submission creates a competing revision, and the visit enters an unresolved state. A coordinator manually selects one competing revision to become the current record.
+
+All original submissions are stored permanently. The current record is always one of the submitted observations, unmodified.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Preservation of scientific intent**: **Strong fit.** The server treats each submission as a complete scientific unit, the exact observation the worker made in the field. No automatic combination means no risk of unintended relationships. A coordinator reviewing the conflict sees both workers' full observations side by side.
+
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden**: **Strong fit.** The logic is straightforward: compare base-revision to current revision, advance or create competing revision. No merge rules to maintain, no special handling for different field types. As fields are added to the schema, there is nothing to maintain. The core algorithm scales linearly with the number of competing revisions, not the number of fields.
+
+- **Review workload**: **Weak fit.** Every concurrent edit surfaces as review work, even edits to unrelated fields. In a team of 24 workers submitting ~6,000 visits per season, if even 5–10% of visits attract independent corrections (a reasonable estimate for field work), that surfaces 300–600 reviews. In a Friday export cycle, two coordinators face a queued review load.
+
+- **Downstream contract clarity**: **Strong fit.** Tablet-sync needs to track base-revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export needs a revision-diff view and must exclude unresolved visits from export. Both are tractable contracts: revision tracking is a common pattern, and exclusion is simpler than conditional merging.
+
+**Summary**: Keeps each submission intact as a scientific unit; requires manual review of all concurrent edits, even unrelated ones; simple maintenance story.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each submission carries the base state (the complete observation the worker started from) and a set of structured changes (updated fields and their new values). The server performs a three-way merge: compare the base against the current record to detect which fields have changed independently, and automatically combine disjoint changes. Overlapping changes (both workers editing the same field) require review.
+
+The system maintains merge rules for each field or field type: scalar fields combine if only one writer changed them; observer lists combine if disjoint; species entries follow specific rules. Fields added in the future require new merge rules. The system stores not just the base and changes, but the merged result and metadata about which changes came from which submission, for provenance.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- **Preservation of scientific intent**: **Partial fit.** Automatically combining field edits works when changes are truly independent—one worker corrects an observer name, another adds a species. But merge rules operate on field names, not scientific meaning. A worker who revises both a species ID and its cover estimate independently from another worker's change to those same fields faces a rule-based merge that may combine them in ways the scientific relationships don't support. The system has no way to know that these two fields are scientifically linked. Over time, as data is merged and re-merged, the audit trail of which worker intended what deteriorates.
+
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden**: **Weak fit.** The merge rules themselves are simple initially (disjoint scalar fields combine). But observer lists and species entries (which are themselves records with multiple properties) require more sophisticated rules. Adding a new field to the schema requires deciding its merge behavior. Fixing a rule that turns out to be wrong requires identifying and potentially re-merging all visits affected by the old rule. The system becomes fragile to schema evolution.
+
+- **Review workload**: **Strong fit.** Only overlapping field edits surface as review work. Unrelated corrections combine automatically and reduce the review queue. In the same scenario (5–10% of visits attract corrections), the review load drops to only the subset where two workers actually edited the same field—potentially 50–100 reviews instead of 300–600.
+
+- **Downstream contract clarity**: **Partial fit.** Tablet-sync must send base snapshots and structured changes, adding complexity to the client. Review-export must track field-level provenance and apply merge rules, making the contract much larger. The merge rules themselves become part of the contract and must be versioned as they evolve.
+
+**Summary**: Reduces review workload by automatically combining unrelated field edits; requires maintaining merge rules and risks combining scientifically related fields the rules don't understand; higher maintenance burden as the system evolves.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions is chosen.**
+
+This decision turns on two criteria weighted as major: **preservation of scientific intent** and **implementation and maintenance burden**. The team's domain is ecological observation—the relationship between field values matters, and incorrectly merging related edits risks corrupting the data. A field-aware merge would require encoded knowledge (a rule) for every meaningful field relationship, and that knowledge would decay as the system is extended.
+
+The review workload cost (300–600 reviews per season) is material but manageable. The team already operates a coordinator review workflow, and Friday exports are a known bottleneck; the choice does not create a new constraint but intersects an existing one. If review volume grows unsustainably, the decision could be revisited, but starting with the simpler, more conservative approach is safer: original submissions are intact, the audit trail is clear, and the coordinator sees the full context of each conflict.
+
+The implementation simplicity is a secondary but real benefit. The core logic is straightforward; the system's behavior is predictable as fields are added; and future maintainers will understand the design without needing to learn a set of merge rules.
+
+**Implications:**
+
+- **Downstream obligation — Tablet-sync**: Tablets must track the current base-revision ID for each visit and display pending competing revisions so workers can see what's waiting for review. When a worker edits a visit, they supply the base-revision ID they started from.
+
+- **Downstream obligation — Review-export**: The review queue must present competing revisions side by side so coordinators can select a winner. A revision-diff view is required. Unresolved visits cannot enter the Friday export; the coordinator must resolve them first or they are excluded from the week's CSV.
+
+- **Foreclosed option**: Field-aware merging could be revisited in a future iteration if review volume becomes unsustainable. The decision does not lock out that path; it sequences conservatively. If evidence shows the team can handle more concurrent edits with automatic merging, a future migration could introduce it. But the migration would be non-trivial: the storage schema and client contracts would shift, and historical data would need re-processing.
